      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Effective electromagnetic shielding requires a continuous conductive enclosure with apertures smaller than roughly λ/20 for the frequency of interest.",
      "evidence": "Standard Faraday‑cage design rules: holes larger than ~5% of wavelength allow significant transmission (e.g., IEEE Std 299).",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "A tin‑foil hat provides only partial coverage and contains apertures (top opening, sides, face, neck) that are many centimeters wide, far larger than λ/20 for typical radio frequencies (e.g., 100 MHz λ≈3 m, λ/20≈15 cm), so net attenuation of broadcast radio, cellular, Wi‑Fi, etc., is negligible (≤10 dB).",
      "evidence": "Skin depth of aluminum at 100 MHz ≈8 µm, so the foil thickness is sufficient to reflect some power, but measurements of a single‑sheet foil patch with large gaps show shielding effectiveness <10 dB (e.g., IEC 61000‑4‑21 test data). The dominant loss is through the large openings, not the material thickness.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "No scientifically verified external signal exists that can directly control human thoughts or convey alien communications, and no known government surveillance relies on a single‑frequency signal that could be blocked by a hat.",
      "evidence": "Neuroscience literature shows thoughts are electrochemical, not radiated EM; no peer‑reviewed reports of “mind‑control radio”. Government intelligence uses a variety of methods, none of which are a single radio band that a hat could block.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Because the hat does not appreciably attenuate the broad spectrum of radio waves and there are no known mind‑control or alien signals to block, none of the specific claims B–G are supported; the only accurate description is that nothing special happens.",
      "evidence": "Combining steps 3 and 4: negligible shielding + non‑existent target signals ⇒ no observable effect.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3, 4]
    }
  ],
